Document (dev.stxt.website): Corporate documents

	Metadata:
		Author: ChatGPT 5.2
		Last modif: 2026-08-22
		
	Header: Corporate documents
	
	Content >>
		Corporate documents must be readable and editable by people and, at the same time,
		consistent, reviewable and processable by programs.
		
		@STXT@ applies to documents that combine, at once:
		
		* narrative text,
		* structure (sections, metadata),
		* and data (fields, lists, states).
		
	Subheader: What kind of documents
	
	Content >>
		Common examples:
		
		* Meeting minutes
		* Status reports (weekly/monthly)
		* Proposals / internal RFCs
		* Policies and procedures
		* Postmortems (incidents)
		* Requirements and decisions (ADR: Architecture Decision Record, architecture decision log)
		
	Subheader: Why @STXT@ here
	
	Content >>
		Common requirements of a corporate document:
		
		* **Multiple authors**: technical and non-technical people edit the same document.
		* **Templates**: every document of a given type follows the same format, without
		  depending on a specific editor.
		* **Review**: readable differences (diff) in Git.
		* **Automation**: extraction of fields (status, owners, dates, actions) without
		  altering the text.
		* **Evolution**: the document format changes over time.
		
		@STXT@ provides:
		
		* Explicit structure through indentation.
		* Free text in `>>` text blocks, with no escape sequences or additional syntax.
		* Optional validation with `@stxt.template` or `@stxt.schema`.
		* Namespaces that separate semantics by document type (`com.acme.minutes`,
		  `com.acme.report`, etc.).
		
	Subheader: Example 1 — Meeting minutes
	
	Content >>
		The minutes combine metadata, agenda, decisions and actions. Free text goes in text
		blocks; processable data, in inline nodes.
		
	Code >>
		Minutes (com.acme.corp.minutes):
			Title: Weekly sync — Platform
			Date: 2026-01-09
			Time: 10:00:00
			Duration minutes: 45
			Meeting id: PLAT-2026-01-09
			Location: Google Meet
			Confidential: false
			
			Attendees:
				Attendee: Joan Costa
				Attendee: Mery Adams
				Attendee: Keyla Brown
			
			Agenda:
				Item:
					Title: Q1 roadmap status
				Item:
					Title: Incidents and risks
				Item:
					Title: Pending decisions
			
			Notes >>
				Executive summary:
				- Roadmap is green, but team capacity still needs to be finalized.
				- Main risk: dependency on an external vendor in module X.
				
				Observations:
				- It is agreed to move Friday’s deployment to Monday.
				- The cost of option B will be reviewed.
			
			Decisions:
				Decision:
					Id: DEC-0142
					Title: Move deployment window to Monday
					Decision status: Approved
					Owner: Platform Team
					Rationale >>
						We reduce operational risk and make support easier on Monday.
						
				Decision:
					Id: DEC-0143
					Title: Freeze non-critical changes in module X
					Decision status: Proposed
					Owner: Reliability
					Rationale >>
						External dependency with high variability in response times.
			
			Action Items:
				Action:
					Id: ACT-0991
					Title: Prepare capacity proposal (Q1)
					Owner: Mery Adams
					Due: 2026-01-16
					Action status: Open
				Action:
					Id: ACT-0992
					Title: Estimate cost of option B
					Owner: Joan Costa
					Due: 2026-01-14
					Action status: In Progress
	
	Subheader: What gets automated with this document
	
	Content >>
		From the document tree, a program can:
		
		* List the open actions and their due dates.
		* Generate a summary for email or chat.
		* Compute metrics: actions per team, deadlines, percentage of actions in state `Done`.
		* Export to other formats (for example, JSON for a dashboard).
		
		At the same time:
		
		* The `Notes >>` and `Rationale >>` blocks preserve the free text without interpreting it.
		
	Subheader: Example 2 — Status report
	
	Content >>
		A status report combines:
		
		* the status as validatable data,
		* the detail as free text,
		* the risks and blockers as processable lists.
		
	Code >>
		Status Report (com.acme.corp.status):
			Period:
				From: 2026-01-05
				To: 2026-01-09
			
			Project: STXT Web
			Owner: Platform Docs
			Status: Green
			
			Summary >>
				Steady progress on the base documentation and consolidation of examples.
				Work remains on tools (CLI and online editor) and use cases.
			
			Progress:
				Item:
					Title: Introduction page
					Done: true
				Item:
					Title: Updated tutorial
					Done: true
				Item:
					Title: Specifications (core/schema/template)
					Done: In Progress
			
			Risks:
				Risk:
					Id: RSK-020
					Level: Medium
					Title: Lack of migration examples
					Mitigation >>
						Create XML/YAML examples and explain equivalences.
			
			Next:
				Item: Close use case pages
				Item: Publish first version of the Java parser
				Item: Prepare examples repository
	
	Subheader: Validation with @stxt.template
	
	Content >>
		A template (`@stxt.template`) defines the minimum structure of the document; the
		validator rejects incomplete documents.
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.corp.minutes
			Description >>
				Minutes: Corporate minutes template
			Structure >>
				Minutes:
					Title: (1)
					Date: (1) DATE
					Time: (?) TIME
					Duration minutes: (?) NATURAL
					Meeting id: (?) 
					Location: (?)
					Confidential: (?) BOOLEAN
					
					Attendees: (?)
						Attendee: (+)
					
					Agenda: (?)
						Item: (*)
							Title: (1) @Title
					
					Notes: (?) TEXT
					
					Decisions: (?)
						Decision: (*)
							Id: (1)
							Title: (1) @Title
							Decision status: (1) ENUM [Proposed, Approved, Rejected]
							Owner: (?)
							Rationale: (?) TEXT
					
					Action Items: (?)
						Action: (*)
							Id: (1) @Id
							Title: (1) @Title
							Owner: (?) @Owner
							Due: (?) DATE
							Action status: (1) ENUM [Open, In Progress, Done, Blocked]
	
	Content >>
		This template:
		
		* Requires the mandatory fields (`Title`, `Date`) with the `(1)` cardinality.
		* Restricts the states with `ENUM`.
		* Declares the free-text fields with the `TEXT` type.
		* Uses the short authoring form: it does not require the `Node` and `Child` nodes of a
		  schema.
		* Reuses `Title`, `Id` and `Owner` through `@Node Name` references: within a namespace a
		  node name identifies **one** node, so its reappearances are referenced instead of
		  redefined.
		* That is why `Decision status` and `Action status` are distinct nodes: their `ENUM`
		  values differ, and a reference cannot change them.
		
	Subheader: Validation with @stxt.schema
	
	Content >>
		The schema (`@stxt.schema`) is the explicit form of the same definition. It is used when:
		
		* There are several producers and consumers of the document.
		* Interoperability between teams or systems is required.
		* There are compliance requirements (audits, controls, archiving).
		
		The equivalent schema is longer; every template compiles to an equivalent schema, so
		it does not need to be written by hand.
		
	Subheader: Practical advantages in a company
	
	Content >>
		* **Diffs in Git**: changes affect specific lines, not binary files.
		* **Editing**: with any text editor.
		* **Standardization**: the template acts as the contract of the format.
		* **Gradual adoption**: a document without validation is valid; the template is added
		  later without changing the syntax.
		* **Integration**: the parser produces a tree that is exported to JSON, XML or YAML or
		  consumed by internal tools.
		
	Subheader: Style recommendations
	
	Content >>
		* Stable node names (“Action Items”, “Decision”, “Action status”…).
		* `>>` text blocks only for long text, context or rationale.
		* Explicit, bounded cardinalities in templates.
		* States as `ENUM` with bounded values.
		* In documents versioned in Git, no automatic reformatting (mass whitespace changes)
		  that hides the changes in the diffs.
